-- FLOAT DIVISION end to end. Expected exit code 29
--
-- `/` was refused on floats until now, and the refusal was principled rather
-- than lazy: a correctly-rounded quotient needs a STICKY BIT, because the exact
-- quotient of two dyadics is in general not a dyadic (`1/3`) and there is
-- nothing exact to hand the rounder. `Once.Float.Arith.divBin` computes enough
-- quotient bits that the rounding position is strictly above the last one and
-- folds "the division was inexact" into that last bit.
--
-- THE GUARD SHIFT IS `+ 3`, NOT `+ 2`, and this program is downstream of that.
-- With one discarded bit the sticky would be folded into the ROUND bit itself
-- and `1.0 / 3.0` answers one ulp high. The module pins that case against the
-- machine; here the whole pipeline runs.
--
-- What this checks that the pins cannot: that the block LOWERS (the recogniser
-- folds `arith.div.float` into an arith block rather than leaving a call to a
-- runtime symbol that does not exist), that it ASSEMBLES on all three targets
-- (`divsd` / `divss` / `fdiv.d`), and that the program reaches its exit code.
--
-- The VALUE is read back by `FloatEmitSpec` out of the effect trace, at each
-- arch's own precision — that is where the answer is checked, not here.

import I.Linux.Syscalls as S
import I.Test.Emit as E

emitQuot : IO Unit
emitQuot = emitF@E (1.0 / 3.0 + 7.0 / 11.0)

main : IO Unit
main = compose exit@S (compose 29 emitQuot)
